 ### Implementation for User Story 1
 
-- [ ] T020 [US1] Implement `_classify_source(path)` in `src/tarx/core.py`: one `os.lstat` per entry, returning `KIND_FILE` for `stat.S_ISREG`, `KIND_DIR` for `stat.S_ISDIR`, and otherwise the skip label from T006. `lstat`, **not** `stat` — classifying a symbolic link as a link rather than as its target is what makes "symbolic links are not followed" true by construction. Hard-link detection is deliberately absent: a file carrying several names is a plain regular file here (FR-015, data-model.md → Classification of a source entry)
-- [ ] T021 [US1] Implement the recursive subtree collector in `src/tarx/core.py` (a helper of `validate_and_plan`) walking one operand with `os.scandir`, classifying each entry via T020, appending `PlannedMember`s to a per-operand list and non-regular entries to `plan.skipped`, and **omitting** any entry whose `os.path.normpath(os.path.abspath(path))` equals the same for `ARCHIVE` — an absolute-string comparison, so it works whether or not the archive exists yet, unlike `os.path.samefile` (FR-019, research.md §7)
-- [ ] T022 [US1] In `src/tarx/core.py`, sort each operand's collected list **once** by full member name as a Python string, then append to `plan.members`, skipping any name already present so the first operand to reach a name keeps its position. Sorting after full collection is what makes `os.scandir`'s reporting order irrelevant; do **not** use `os.walk` with `dirnames.sort()`, which emits a subdirectory's contents after all its siblings and would place `d/b/c.txt` after `d/z.txt` (FR-020, FR-026, research.md §6)
-- [ ] T023 [US1] Implement the happy path of `validate_and_plan(archive, paths, overwrite)` in `src/tarx/core.py`: compute the archive's absolute path once, iterate the `PATH` operands in the order given, derive each operand's member name with `_member_name` (T010), collect its subtree (T021–T022), and return `(plan, [])`. This function **writes nothing** — not one byte, not one directory (FR-016)
-- [ ] T024 [US1] Handle the operand that normalises to `"."` in `src/tarx/core.py`: contribute its entries under their plain relative names and **no** member for the operand itself, because FR-004 requires every member name to be a relative path with no leading `./` and `.` is not such a name. Do not refuse it — `.` does not resolve outside the current directory, so FR-014 does not reach it (research.md §5)
-- [ ] T025 [US1] Implement `write_archive(archive, plan)` in `src/tarx/core.py`: `tarfile.open(archive, _archive_mode(archive))`, then for each planned member in `plan.members` order build a `tarfile.TarInfo` **by hand** — setting `name`, `type` (`tarfile.REGTYPE` for files, `tarfile.DIRTYPE` for directories), `size`, `mode` and `mtime` — and write it with `addfile(info, fileobj)` for files and `addfile(info)` for directories. Do **not** call `TarFile.add()`: it keeps an inode cache and stores the second name of a hard-linked file as a zero-byte `LNKTYPE` member, which FR-015 forbids and which `001-tarx-cli`'s `extract` would silently skip (measured, research.md §4); it also appends a trailing `/` to directory names and re-`stat`s every entry
-- [ ] T026 [US1] Stream each regular file's bytes in `write_archive` in `src/tarx/core.py` by opening it and passing the file object to `addfile`, letting `tarfile`'s internal fixed-size buffer do the copying. Never read a whole file into memory and never build the archive in memory — measured peak additional allocation is 44 KiB for plain tar and 519 KiB for gzip on a 40 MB file, neither growing with file size (SC-010, research.md §9)
-- [ ] T027 [US1] Return `N` from `write_archive` in `src/tarx/core.py` as `sum(1 for m in plan.members.values() if m.kind == KIND_FILE)` — derived from the plan that was actually written, never counted independently, so the reported count cannot drift from what the archive holds (FR-008, SC-003)
